{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "huldah-scroll",
  "text": "They found a lost scroll, the king panicked, and the men on the payroll went and asked a woman what it meant.\n\nSecond Kings twenty-two. They're renovating the temple, and Hilkiah the high priest finds the book of the law. Finds it. In the temple. That is its own scandal.\n\nShaphan the scribe reads it to King Josiah. And the king rends his clothes, because he can hear how far the nation has drifted.\n\nSo he needs a ruling. And he says, \"Go ye, enquire of the Lord for me, and for the people, and for all Judah, concerning the words of this book.\"\n\nNow think about who is standing in that room. A high priest. A scribe. A whole religious establishment on salary.\n\nAnd they go to Huldah the prophetess.\n\nFive men, sent by a king, to a woman, for the authoritative reading of Scripture.\n\nAnd she does not soften it. She opens with, \"Thus saith the Lord God of Israel.\" Judgment on the nation. And then mercy for Josiah personally, because, she says, thine heart was tender.\n\nHer reading became national policy. The whole reformation that follows runs on what she said.",
